// CHTM Academy question bank, units 1 to 3. All questions are original management scenarios.
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u1l1
{id:"u1l1-1",t:"u1l1",q:"A clinical director asks you to fund a $4,100 replacement vital signs monitor, which clears the hospital's capitalization threshold. Which budget does the request belong in and who approves it?",a:["The operating budget, approved by the manager","The capital budget, approved by the capital committee and senior leadership","The operating budget, approved by finance as an exception","The clinical department's supply budget"],c:1,e:"An item with a long useful life whose price clears the capitalization threshold is capital. Capital money is rationed across the whole hospital, so the capital or technology committee and senior leadership approve it, not the department manager."},
{id:"u1l1-2",t:"u1l1",q:"Your hospital uses distributed budgeting, so contracts and parts sit in each clinical cost center. A vendor has missed its response time repeatedly. What is the BEST structural recommendation?",a:["Ask each clinical manager to track response times themselves","Cancel the contract and move to time and materials immediately","Move to consolidated budgeting so HTM holds the equipment dollars and allocates them back from CMMS reports","Report the vendor to purchasing and take no further action"],c:2,e:"Under distributed budgeting nobody who holds the invoice has the data to hold the vendor to its terms. Consolidation puts the equipment dollars under HTM and allocates them back from CMMS reports, so the payer can withhold payment until service reports arrive."},
{id:"u1l1-3",t:"u1l1",q:"A technician needs an $18,000 unplanned repair on a device that is out of contract, and there is no budget line left. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Order the repair and explain the variance at year end","Charge it to another cost center that has money available","Defer the repair until the next fiscal year","Request an exception from finance in writing before the work is ordered"],c:3,e:"An unbudgeted cost must be authorized by finance, in writing, before the commitment is made. Spending first and explaining later leaves an unapproved commitment, and quietly charging another cost center corrupts that department's cost history."},
{id:"u1l1-4",t:"u1l1",q:"Your hospital's fiscal year begins on 1 October. When should the budget for that year normally be built?",a:["Three to six months before the fiscal year starts","In the first month of the new fiscal year","At the end of the preceding calendar year","Whenever finance issues the first monthly report"],c:0,e:"Budgets are typically built three to six months before the fiscal year starts, which means the cost history, contract renewal dates, and replacement plan must be maintained all year rather than assembled in a rush."},
{id:"u1l1-5",t:"u1l1",q:"A device needs a $14,000 assembly every eighteen months, so last year's actual spending shows nothing on that line. What is the MOST appropriate way to handle it in the budget?",a:["Budget nothing and treat it as an exception when it happens","Budget it on its own recurrence interval and agree with the chief financial officer how the cost is carried","Spread it evenly across every account in the department","Ask the clinical department to pay for it from its supply budget"],c:1,e:"A large cost that recurs on a long interval must be budgeted on that interval rather than from last year's actual. The chief financial officer decides how such an irregular cost is carried, and asking before the fiscal year starts protects the manager twice."},

// u1l2
{id:"u1l2-1",t:"u1l2",q:"A technician buys a $900 pump board and the requisition posts it to a generic supplies account rather than to the device's cost center. Which consequence matters MOST to the manager?",a:["The invoice will be paid late","The purchase order will need a second approval","The device's cost history understates the true cost, so the replacement decision rests on a false number","The part will not be covered by warranty"],c:2,e:"Miscoding corrupts the asset's cost history, prevents the clinical cost center from being charged, and makes the cost of service ratio for that model impossible to calculate, which removes the evidence a contract review needs."},
{id:"u1l2-2",t:"u1l2",q:"Where in the general ledger does a service contract payment to an imaging vendor normally post?",a:["Maintenance and repairs","Capital","Travel and education","Purchased maintenance and repair"],c:3,e:"Parts and in-house repair post to maintenance and repairs, while contracts and vendor labor post to purchased maintenance and repair. Keeping them separate is what lets a manager compare in-house cost against vendor cost."},
{id:"u1l2-3",t:"u1l2",q:"You raise a purchase order for a $6,000 assembly on the first of the month. What happens to your available budget balance before any invoice arrives?",a:["The $6,000 is encumbered, so the available balance drops immediately","Nothing changes until the invoice is paid","The budget is increased by the amount committed","The amount posts as revenue until the part is received"],c:0,e:"An encumbrance reserves committed money the moment the purchase order is raised, so two managers cannot spend the same dollar. The accrual then books the expense in the month the goods or work arrived."},
{id:"u1l2-4",t:"u1l2",q:"A $12,000 training program for two technicians is approved. How is it treated in the department's accounts?",a:["Capital, depreciated over the technicians' remaining service","An expense in travel and education, because knowledge cannot be depreciated","Capital if it exceeds the capitalization threshold","Charged to the clinical departments that benefit"],c:1,e:"Training is always an expense and posts to travel and education, because it cannot be depreciated. Only long-lived assets above the capitalization threshold are capital."},
{id:"u1l2-5",t:"u1l2",q:"Finance asks you to explain three six-digit account numbers in your spending report. What is the BEST habit for avoiding this conversation in future?",a:["Keep your own spending categories and translate them when asked","Ask finance to rename the accounts to match your categories","Budget and report in the hospital's own chart of accounts from the start","Report only totals so the detail never comes up"],c:2,e:"The chart of accounts is the hospital's list, and every review and question happens in those numbers. A manager who tracks spending in invented categories starts every finance conversation with a translation argument."},

// u1l3
{id:"u1l3-1",t:"u1l3",q:"A monthly report shows maintenance and repairs budgeted at $12,000 with actual spending of $14,400. What is the variance and its percent?",a:["$2,400 favorable, 20 percent","$26,400 unfavorable, 120 percent","$2,400 unfavorable, 16.7 percent","$2,400 unfavorable, 20 percent"],c:3,e:"Variance is actual minus budget: 14,400 minus 12,000 is 2,400. Percent is variance divided by budget: 2,400 / 12,000 x 100 is 20 percent. On an expense line, over budget is unfavorable."},
{id:"u1l3-2",t:"u1l3",q:"Purchased maintenance shows a large unfavorable variance for March, but the year-to-date column is exactly on plan. What does this MOST likely indicate?",a:["A timing variance, because a contract invoice landed in a different month than budgeted","A real variance that needs a budget amendment","A coding error that must be reversed","An increase in the contract price"],c:0,e:"A gap in one month that disappears in the year-to-date column is a timing variance and closes by itself. Always read the year-to-date column before calling a variance real."},
{id:"u1l3-3",t:"u1l3",q:"At the half year your parts line is 18 percent favorable while overtime has doubled. What is the MOST appropriate reading before you report the favorable line as good news?",a:["Report the savings and request that the parts budget be reduced","Check the scheduled maintenance completion rate and the backlog, because the two lines may be one story","Move the savings to the training line","Assume the parts prices fell and leave it alone"],c:1,e:"A large favorable expense variance usually means work did not happen. A favorable parts line beside doubled overtime suggests too few people, slipped scheduled maintenance, and hours bought at premium rates."},
{id:"u1l3-4",t:"u1l3",q:"Finance asks for a written explanation of an unfavorable travel and education line. Which structure does leadership expect?",a:["A statement that the line was overspent, with an apology","A list of every transaction in the account","What the variance is, why it happened, and what you will do about it","A request to move the budget from another line"],c:2,e:"A variance explanation is three parts in one order every time: what, why, and what you will do. It never consists only of the word overspent, and it never promises a recovery the numbers cannot show."},
{id:"u1l3-5",t:"u1l3",q:"Your year-to-date actual spending after six months of a twelve-month year is $672,300. What is the straight run-rate projection for the full year?",a:["$672,300","$1,120,500","$1,008,450","$1,344,600"],c:3,e:"Divide year-to-date actual by months elapsed and multiply by twelve: 672,300 / 6 is 112,050 a month, and 112,050 x 12 is 1,344,600. Then adjust for known events, and state the adjustments."},

// u1l4
{id:"u1l4-1",t:"u1l4",q:"A $1,000,000 system has a ten-year useful life and no salvage value. Using straight-line depreciation, what is its book value after three years?",a:["$700,000","$1,000,000","$300,000","$100,000"],c:0,e:"Straight-line depreciation is (cost minus salvage) / useful life, which is 100,000 a year. Book value is cost minus accumulated depreciation, so after three years it is 1,000,000 minus 300,000."},
{id:"u1l4-2",t:"u1l4",q:"A clinical director argues that a fully depreciated analyzer must be replaced because its book value is now zero. What is the BEST response?",a:["Agree, since a zero book value means the device is at the end of its life","Explain that book value is an accounting position and replacement rests on support, failure trend, parts availability, and clinical need","Agree, and submit the capital request immediately","Explain that the device must be disposed of to clear the ledger"],c:1,e:"Book value is cost minus accumulated depreciation and says nothing about condition or remaining useful life. A fully depreciated device may be safe, accurate, and years from replacement, while a half-depreciated one may already be unsupportable."},
{id:"u1l4-3",t:"u1l4",q:"Which statement shows assets, liabilities, and equity on a single date, with equipment carried at book value?",a:["The income statement","The cash flow statement","The balance sheet","The monthly budget report"],c:2,e:"The balance sheet is a snapshot on one day and it balances: assets equal liabilities plus equity. The income statement covers a period and shows revenue minus expenses equals net income."},
{id:"u1l4-4",t:"u1l4",q:"A vendor invoice arrives for quarterly contract service, but no service report has been filed in the CMMS. What should the manager do?",a:["Approve the invoice, since the contract price is fixed","Forward it to the clinical department to approve","Approve it and ask for the report at renewal","Withhold approval until the service report arrives, because approving the payable confirms the work was done and documented"],c:3,e:"Approving an invoice in accounts payable is the manager's confirmation that the work was performed and documented. Withholding approval until the service report arrives is the leverage consolidation was designed to give."},
{id:"u1l4-5",t:"u1l4",q:"Your hospital reports positive net income, yet an approved capital item is still waiting. Which statement explains why?",a:["The cash flow statement, because reported profit is not money in the bank","The balance sheet, because equity is too low","The income statement, because expenses exceeded revenue","The business activity report, because volumes fell"],c:0,e:"The cash flow statement tracks money in and out, split into operating, investing, and financing. A hospital can show net income and still lack cash this quarter, which is a real answer rather than an excuse."},

// u1l5
{id:"u1l5-1",t:"u1l5",q:"A $25,000 investment in training and tools avoids $90,000 of contract cost in its first year. What is the return on investment?",a:["360 percent","260 percent","72 percent","28 percent"],c:1,e:"ROI is (gain minus cost) / cost x 100. Here (90,000 minus 25,000) / 25,000 x 100 is 260 percent, meaning each dollar returned itself and $1.60 besides inside the year."},
{id:"u1l5-2",t:"u1l5",q:"A proposed project costs $240,000 and produces a net annual benefit of $38,000. What is the payback period?",a:["About 1.6 years","About 9.1 years","About 6.3 years","About 15.8 years"],c:2,e:"Payback is cost divided by annual savings: 240,000 / 38,000 is about 6.3 years. Payback asks how long the hospital's money is at risk, while ROI asks how much the investment returns."},
{id:"u1l5-3",t:"u1l5",q:"System A costs $410,000 to buy and has a ten-year total cost of ownership of $792,000. System B costs $370,000 to buy and has a ten-year total cost of ownership of $959,000. Which recommendation is correct and why?",a:["System B, because the purchase price is lower","System B, because service costs are not part of the capital decision","Either, since the difference is within normal variation","System A, because its ten-year total cost of ownership is lower despite the higher purchase price"],c:3,e:"Total cost of ownership covers purchase, installation, service, parts, training, software, consumables, and disposal across a stated horizon. A lower sticker price that carries higher lifetime cost loses, and the horizon must always be stated."},
{id:"u1l5-4",t:"u1l5",q:"In which order should a business justification present its content?",a:["Problem, options, costs, benefits, risks, recommendation","Recommendation, costs, problem, options, benefits, risks","Costs, benefits, problem, recommendation, options, risks","Options, recommendation, problem, risks, benefits, costs"],c:0,e:"A justification runs problem, options, costs, benefits, risks, then recommendation, and it always says what happens if nothing is funded. Doing nothing is described honestly as one of the options."},
{id:"u1l5-5",t:"u1l5",q:"You want uptime and clinician satisfaction counted as benefits in a proposal. What is the BEST way to state them?",a:["Describe them as better service, since they cannot be priced","State each as a measurable change from a named baseline, with the date you will report it","Leave them out, because only dollar benefits are accepted","Convert them into dollars using an assumed value per hour"],c:1,e:"Soft benefits survive a meeting only when they carry a measurement: a baseline, a target, the instrument, and a reporting date. Stated as better service they add nothing, and inflated dollar conversions destroy credibility."},

// u1l6
{id:"u1l6-1",t:"u1l6",q:"Your review shows one imaging vendor at a cost of service ratio of 18.2 percent while a comparable vendor runs 9.5 percent. What is the MOST appropriate next step?",a:["Insource the high-cost equipment immediately","Switch the equipment to time and materials this month","Review that contract at renewal with the comparison in hand, and first confirm whether parts, training, and service software can be bought","Report the ratio to the committee and take no action"],c:2,e:"A ratio comparison raises a question rather than settling it. Insourcing depends on whether parts, training, and service software are sold to equipment owners, so the honest output is sometimes a question with a date on it."},
{id:"u1l6-2",t:"u1l6",q:"Over three years your scheduled maintenance completion held near 96 percent while backlog, total time to repair, and overtime all deteriorated and assets per technician rose from 247 to 318. What does this support?",a:["A request to lower the completion rate target","A conclusion that the department is performing well","A recommendation to reduce the inventory count","A staffing request, because compliance was bought with premium-rate hours"],c:3,e:"Several deteriorating measures plus one flat measure make a single argument. A completion rate held only by doubling overtime means the department is buying its compliance at a premium rate, which is a staffing case rather than a success."},
{id:"u1l6-3",t:"u1l6",q:"Which measure benchmarks MOST reliably between two different hospitals?",a:["Cost of service ratio","Assets supported per technician","Total labor hours per device","Number of open work orders"],c:0,e:"Cost of service ratio compares reasonably because both hospitals have the two numbers and the ratio cancels size. Counts such as assets per technician and labor hours per device compare badly, because every hospital counts its inventory differently."},
{id:"u1l6-4",t:"u1l6",q:"How many comparable periods does a defensible trend require, and what else must stay constant?",a:["Two periods, with the data source constant","At least three comparable periods, with the definition of the measure constant","Twelve periods, with the staffing level constant","One period, compared against an industry figure"],c:1,e:"A trend needs at least three comparable periods and a definition that does not change. Counting the same thing a different way and reporting the difference as improvement is the most common self-inflicted error."},
{id:"u1l6-5",t:"u1l6",q:"You are presenting an operational analysis to senior leadership. What should the one-page summary contain?",a:["Every chart you produced, with the detail in front","A description of the department's workload and staffing history","The number, the trend, the cause, the recommendation, and what you need from them","The raw CMMS extract with totals highlighted"],c:2,e:"Leadership reads cost, risk, and capacity. One page carrying the number, the trend, the cause, a recommendation, and the ask, with the detail attached behind it, and the same metrics every time so the comparison is the message."},

// u2l1
{id:"u2l1-1",t:"u2l1",q:"A manufacturer announces that a model you own reaches end of life in eighteen months. What does that announcement actually mean?",a:["The devices become unsafe on that date and must be removed from service","The devices must be reported to the accreditor","The manufacturer must still supply parts for a further five years","The manufacturer will stop supporting the model, which is a planning deadline rather than a safety verdict"],c:3,e:"End of life is a commercial decision to stop supporting a model. It does not mean the device is unsafe or unrepairable, and no law requires a manufacturer to supply parts or support for any number of years afterward."},
{id:"u2l1-2",t:"u2l1",q:"You have twelve replacement candidates and funding for four. What is the MOST appropriate way to bring this to the capital committee?",a:["One ranked list scored against published weights, with what the hospital carries if the lower items are not funded","Twelve separate requests, each with its own clinical case","A list in order of device age","The four you consider most urgent, with the rest withheld"],c:0,e:"A committee can approve a line in a ranked list and cannot adjudicate twelve separate arguments. Publish the factors and weights before scoring, rank, and state the consequence of leaving the unfunded items in service."},
{id:"u2l1-3",t:"u2l1",q:"Which input to a useful-life decision provides the longest and cheapest advance warning that replacement is coming?",a:["A failure that cancels clinical cases","The manufacturer's end-of-support notice, often published years ahead","A calibration that will no longer hold","An increase in parts lead times"],c:1,e:"Support-withdrawal notices arrive years ahead, which makes them the earliest warning available. Rising cost per device needs two or three years of data, and a failure that cancels cases provides no warning at all."},
{id:"u2l1-4",t:"u2l1",q:"Two identical analyzers were bought the same day, one running three shifts in a busy core lab and one running intermittently in a clinic. How should an industry useful-life schedule be used?",a:["Apply the schedule's figure to both, since they are the same model","Replace both on the schedule's date to keep the fleet standard","Use the schedule to set depreciation and start the conversation, then decide from each device's own failure and cost data","Ignore the schedule entirely"],c:2,e:"A published life-expectancy schedule is a national average by asset type, widely used to set depreciation. Workload, environment, and failure history decide the actual device, so the schedule is an input rather than an answer."},
{id:"u2l1-5",t:"u2l1",q:"A new director asks why one device was replaced ahead of another two years ago. What should the file behind each replacement line hold?",a:["The purchase order and the invoice","The manufacturer's brochure and quotation","A copy of the capital committee agenda","The asset record, cost history, failure history, support notice, clinical statement of need, and the dated score sheet"],c:3,e:"The file must answer how the decision was made. Asset record, cost and failure history, the written support notice, the clinical statement of need, and the score sheet with its date are what satisfy an auditor, a surveyor, or a successor."},

// u2l2
{id:"u2l2-1",t:"u2l2",q:"A vendor offers a service contract and emphasizes everything it covers. Which part of the agreement decides your real cost?",a:["The exclusion list","The coverage list","The annual price","The renewal clause"],c:0,e:"Every excluded item becomes an unbudgeted repair later in the year. Batteries, accessories, probes, glassware, major assemblies, abuse, after-hours work, software, and travel are the usual exclusions, and each is worth asking about by name."},
{id:"u2l2-2",t:"u2l2",q:"A $180,000 annual contract covers a system that cost $1,000,000. What is the cost of service ratio?",a:["5.6 percent","18 percent","1.8 percent","180 percent"],c:1,e:"Cost of service ratio is annual service cost divided by acquisition cost times 100: 180,000 / 1,000,000 x 100 is 18 percent. Compare it against the department's own ratio rather than only against a published band."},
{id:"u2l2-3",t:"u2l2",q:"A vendor representative promised extra training seats verbally during negotiation, but the signed contract does not mention them. What governs?",a:["The verbal promise, since it was made during negotiation","The email thread, which has equal weight","The signed contract, because an entire-agreement clause makes it the whole deal","Whatever the vendor's regional manager now agrees to"],c:2,e:"An entire-agreement clause means the signed contract is the whole deal, and a term can run as long as ten years. Nothing said in a meeting or an email survives it, so every commitment must be written into the agreement before signature."},
{id:"u2l2-4",t:"u2l2",q:"A renewal notice arrives sixty days out at a six percent increase, and purchasing wants an answer. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Accept the increase to avoid a gap in coverage","Move the equipment to time and materials","Ask the vendor to justify the increase in writing","Pull the department's own records of calls, response times met and missed, scheduled visits delivered, parts consumed, and downtime"],c:3,e:"A contract you have not measured is a contract you renew blind. The department's CMMS records of response, delivery, parts consumed, and downtime turn a renewal into a negotiation rather than a signature."},
{id:"u2l2-5",t:"u2l2",q:"Which pair of response commitments should a service contract state separately, each with a remedy when missed?",a:["Time to a phone response and time to on-site arrival","Time to quote and time to invoice","Time to parts delivery and time to payment","Time to training and time to software update"],c:0,e:"Phone response and on-site arrival are different obligations and must be stated separately with a remedy for each. A promise to respond promptly with no stated remedy creates no obligation at all."},

// u2l3
{id:"u2l3-1",t:"u2l3",q:"Under which service arrangement does the hospital effectively self-insure, paying per call for labor, parts, and travel with nothing committed in advance?",a:["Full service contract","Time and materials","Shared risk","Preventive only"],c:1,e:"Time and materials commits nothing in advance and bills each call, which makes the hospital its own insurer. Managed well on a low-risk fleet it can cut cost by as much as sixty percent against a full contract."},
{id:"u2l3-2",t:"u2l3",q:"A sales representative offers an extended service agreement at the moment of a capital purchase, with no HTM involvement. What is the BEST advice to leadership?",a:["Accept it, because buying service with the device simplifies administration","Accept it for the first year only","Decline it and keep the service decision with HTM, because the margin on such agreements can run as high as ninety percent and cancellation usually carries a penalty","Negotiate the price down by half and accept it"],c:2,e:"A point-of-sale or extended warranty is the worst value on the list: the seller's margin can reach ninety percent and cancellation usually carries a penalty. The moment to stop one is before the purchase closes."},
{id:"u2l3-3",t:"u2l3",q:"Nine units of one model are in service, one senior technician is trained on it, and the manufacturer sells parts and service keys to equipment owners. What is the BEST recommendation?",a:["Full insourcing, since parts and a trained technician exist","Time and materials, because the volume is low","A full service contract, because only one technician is trained","A first-look arrangement with a second technician trained, because one person is not a program"],c:3,e:"Parts access and response time favor in-house work, but a single trained technician is a single point of failure. First look with a second seat funded captures most of the saving while covering leave and turnover."},
{id:"u2l3-4",t:"u2l3",q:"Which arrangement has in-house staff respond first and calls the vendor only when staff cannot complete the work?",a:["First look","Shared risk","Parts only","Multi-vendor service"],c:0,e:"Under first look the hospital carries the routine calls and the vendor carries the hard ones. It usually prices below full service while keeping a route to the manufacturer for work beyond the shop's reach."},
{id:"u2l3-5",t:"u2l3",q:"Your department insources a model, then the manufacturer stops selling parts to equipment owners. What should happen to the arrangement?",a:["Keep the arrangement, since the cost comparison still favors in-house work","End in-house service for that model, because parts access is the decisive factor whatever the cost comparison says","Buy parts from brokers to keep the program running","Ask the clinical department to absorb the longer downtime"],c:1,e:"Parts access is the factor that can end an in-house program on its own. Without a legitimate parts route the comparison is meaningless, and brokered parts with no traceability are not an acceptable substitute on clinical equipment."},

// u2l4
{id:"u2l4-1",t:"u2l4",q:"A blower assembly is needed for a ventilator. A broker offers one at $700 with no warranty and no traceability, well below every other source. What should the manager do?",a:["Buy it, because the saving is substantial and the device will be tested afterward","Buy it and record the risk in the work order","Refuse it and buy from a source that can document what the part is, because this is life support","Buy it only if the technician inspects it first"],c:2,e:"Quality, warranty, and traceability outrank price on clinical equipment, and an untraceable part in a life-support device is a finding waiting to happen. Price is the last column you read on a critical device, not the first."},
{id:"u2l4-2",t:"u2l4",q:"An item is used once a year, has a three-week lead time, and four are on the shelf. What does that stock position indicate?",a:["Correct stocking for the lead time","A consignment arrangement is in place","Understocking, given the long lead time","Overstocking: four years of supply with annual turns of 0.25"],c:3,e:"Turns is annual usage divided by stock held, so four units against one a year is 0.25 turns. Low turns is money sitting on a shelf becoming obsolete, and a single bad month five years ago is the usual cause."},
{id:"u2l4-3",t:"u2l4",q:"Your shop raised twenty-six separate purchase orders last month for parts totaling $3,180. What is the BEST improvement?",a:["Consolidate into monthly or quarterly orders, because each purchase order carries roughly $100 of hospital overhead","Negotiate a larger discount with the supplier","Move to a consignment arrangement for every item","Switch to a different supplier with lower prices"],c:0,e:"Raising one purchase order carries roughly $100 of hospital cost, so twenty-six orders added about $2,600 of overhead to $3,180 of parts. Consolidation usually saves more than a negotiated part discount returns."},
{id:"u2l4-4",t:"u2l4",q:"A safety analyzer is found to have been out of calibration for four months. What is the MOST significant consequence?",a:["The analyzer must be replaced","Every record made with it during that period is invalidated and may require re-testing","The calibration contract must be renegotiated","The technician who used it needs retraining"],c:1,e:"A reading taken with out-of-calibration test equipment invalidates the record it produced, which can mean re-testing a fleet and explaining it to a surveyor. Calibration is bought with the instrument, on a schedule, traceable to the national standards kept by NIST, with certificates retained."},
{id:"u2l4-5",t:"u2l4",q:"A technician telephones a vendor to order a $2,400 part and the vendor ships it without a purchase order. What is the audit problem?",a:["The part may be the wrong one","The price was not negotiated","The hospital holds a commitment that skipped the approval, purchase order, and receiving steps the policy requires","The invoice will arrive late"],c:2,e:"The purchasing policy sequence is specification, quotes, approvals, purchase order before the goods arrive, receiving against the order, then invoice matching. A verbal order leaves an unapproved commitment, and that is the finding rather than the price."},

// u2l5
{id:"u2l5-1",t:"u2l5",q:"A seven-year-old system has a book value of $120,000, a replacement cost of $520,000, dealer quotes near $40,000, and a trade-in offer of $25,000. What is its fair market value?",a:["$120,000, the book value","$25,000, the trade-in credit","$520,000, the replacement cost","About $40,000, from current market evidence"],c:3,e:"Fair market value is what a willing buyer would pay a willing seller today, established from completed sales, dealer and broker quotes, auction results, or an appraisal. It is neither book value nor replacement cost, and a trade-in credit is usually below it."},
{id:"u2l5-2",t:"u2l5",q:"You plan to sell an asset for $40,000 while its book value is $120,000. What must happen before the device moves?",a:["Finance must agree to the write-off of the $80,000 of remaining book value","Nothing; the sale proceeds are recorded as revenue","The clinical department must approve the sale price","The accreditor must be notified"],c:0,e:"Disposing of an asset still being depreciated writes off its remaining book value, which is a hit finance must agree to in advance. Waiting until an asset is fully depreciated removes the write-off entirely."},
{id:"u2l5-3",t:"u2l5",q:"An operating lease on a monitoring system reaches the end of its term and the devices go back to the lessor. What is HTM's critical duty?",a:["Obtain a final service report from the lessor","Wipe or destroy the media on any device that stored patient information before it leaves","Arrange a trade-in credit toward the replacement","Transfer the warranty to the replacement devices"],c:1,e:"A leased device returned with patient data still on it is a breach, and the lessor will not raise it. Often only HTM knows the data is there, which makes the wipe or media destruction a standing requirement on every disposal route."},
{id:"u2l5-4",t:"u2l5",q:"What is the correct order of preference for recovering value from a device leaving service?",a:["Donate, recycle, sell, redistribute","Sell, recycle, redistribute, donate","Redistribute internally, sell, donate, recycle","Recycle, donate, redistribute, sell"],c:2,e:"Redistributing inside the organization keeps the full remaining use value, then selling, then donating, then recycling. A trade-in is convenient and usually the lowest cash value available, so price the open market first."},
{id:"u2l5-5",t:"u2l5",q:"Which items must the disposal record carry before an asset is closed out of the inventory and the ledger?",a:["The clinical department's acknowledgment","The asset number and the date only","The purchase order and the original invoice","The asset identification, reason, approval, book value, data wipe method, decontamination, environmental handling, method of disposal, date, and signature"],c:3,e:"A complete disposal record names the asset, the method, the date, the wipe, the decontamination, the environmental route, and the signatures. The CMMS record is then closed rather than deleted, because a recall or an audit may still name that serial number."},

// u2l6
{id:"u2l6-1",t:"u2l6",q:"A surgeon has selected a system, the vendor has quoted it, and the capital committee meets in nine days. You were copied as a courtesy. What is HTM's role?",a:["Consult early, evaluate, and recommend, without deciding alone, and work to be in the conversation months earlier","Approve or reject the purchase on technical grounds","Decline to comment, since the clinical choice has been made","Negotiate the price directly with the vendor"],c:0,e:"The clinical department owns the need, finance owns the money, and leadership owns the decision. HTM owns the technical truth about whether the hospital can run, service, secure, and afford the device for its whole life."},
{id:"u2l6-2",t:"u2l6",q:"A vendor supplies a list of reference sites for a system under evaluation. What should the manager do with it?",a:["File it as evidence that references were provided","Contact the references directly, and also search what the public device report database already holds on the model","Ask the vendor to summarize what the references said","Share it with the clinical department to follow up"],c:1,e:"A reference list supplied by the vendor and never called is worse than none, because the file implies a check that did not happen. Contact them yourself and record what MAUDE already holds on the model."},
{id:"u2l6-3",t:"u2l6",q:"A relative of yours works for one of three bidders on a major purchase. What is the MOST appropriate action?",a:["Say nothing, since you will score fairly","Ask the relative not to discuss the bid with you","Disclose the relationship in writing and recuse yourself from scoring","Remove that bidder from the process"],c:2,e:"Conflicts are managed by disclosure and recusal under the hospital's policy, not by private assurance. The test is whether the decision would look defensible written down, because one day the file will be read by somebody who was not there."},
{id:"u2l6-4",t:"u2l6",q:"Which element belongs in the evaluation record and is MOST often missing when a purchase is rushed?",a:["The vendor's brochure","The invoice terms","The delivery date","The cybersecurity review, with the MDS2, the operating system support horizon, the patch method, and remote access"],c:3,e:"A purchase driven by a clinical selection and a quote usually skips the cybersecurity review, which is where the MDS2, the operating system support horizon, the patch method, authentication, encryption, and remote access get settled before the hospital is committed."},
{id:"u2l6-5",t:"u2l6",q:"What should appear in the first lines of a recommendation going to a capital committee?",a:["The decision requested, the money, and the date it is needed","The clinical background and the history of the request","A full description of each option","The vendor's references and the service strategy"],c:0,e:"A committee can approve a line and cannot adjudicate an essay. Put the decision requested, the amount, and the timing first, follow with the options including doing nothing, and attach the detail behind the summary."},

// u3l1
{id:"u3l1-1",t:"u3l1",q:"The risk manager asks for the department's top five risks by Friday. Which document should already exist to answer that in minutes?",a:["The preventive maintenance schedule","The risk register, with a statement, category, likelihood, consequence, score, controls, owner, action, and dates per line","The equipment inventory report","Last year's survey findings"],c:1,e:"A risk register is one maintained list carrying the statement, category, likelihood and consequence, score, existing controls, a named owner, an action with a date, and a review date. Without owners and dates it is a list of complaints."},
{id:"u3l1-2",t:"u3l1",q:"Equipment in an alternative maintenance program turns out never to have been eligible for it. Which risk category does that fall into FIRST?",a:["Financial risk","Operations risk","Regulatory risk","Emergency preparedness risk"],c:2,e:"A survey finding on maintenance records, an unreported adverse event, or equipment placed in an alternative program that was not eligible are regulatory risks. The categories overlap, and naming every category that applies is often what gets a risk funded."},
{id:"u3l1-3",t:"u3l1",q:"An exam light bulb failure scores likelihood 5 and consequence 1, while a defibrillator failing at a code scores likelihood 1 and consequence 5. Both score 5. What does that teach?",a:["Both risks warrant the same response","Low-likelihood risks can be removed from the register","The scoring scale is wrong and should be changed","A score ranks work but never replaces judgment, so the consequence dimension is read on its own"],c:3,e:"Identical products can describe opposite situations. Spares on a cart answer the bulb; the defibrillator is tested every time and never run to failure. Read the consequence column separately before accepting any ranked list."},
{id:"u3l1-4",t:"u3l1",q:"You recommended a control for a known equipment risk and the recommendation was refused. What should you do?",a:["Escalate in writing, naming what you recommended, and keep the copy","Accept the decision and close the register entry","Implement the control anyway from the department budget","Record it as an accepted risk with no further action"],c:0,e:"Escalate when a device has harmed a patient, when a Class I recall names equipment you own, when a control you recommended has been refused, or when the risk exceeds your authority or budget. Escalate in writing and keep the copy."},
{id:"u3l1-5",t:"u3l1",q:"Which source of risk information is the cheapest data a department will ever get and the most often discarded?",a:["Accreditation survey findings","Near misses","Vendor service reports","The annual inventory count"],c:1,e:"Near misses describe events that did not reach a patient, which means the information arrives with no harm attached. A department that does not collect them loses the warnings it was given for free."},

// u3l2
{id:"u3l2-1",t:"u3l2",q:"A surveyor asks why identical pumps are inspected annually when the manual says every six months. What is the only acceptable answer?",a:["The department has always used that interval","The manufacturer's interval is only a recommendation","A documented, risk-based decision made by qualified personnel, with the evidence on file","The pumps are low risk because they rarely fail"],c:2,e:"Any change away from the manufacturer's recommendations must sit inside a documented alternative program, decided by qualified personnel on a risk assessment, with the methodology and the evidence per equipment type on file."},
{id:"u3l2-2",t:"u3l2",q:"Which equipment may NOT be placed in an alternative equipment maintenance program?",a:["Infusion pumps and physiological monitors","Any device on the critical equipment list","Sterilizers and anesthesia machines","Imaging and radiologic equipment, medical laser devices, and new equipment without enough maintenance history"],c:3,e:"Imaging and radiologic equipment, whether diagnostic or therapeutic, medical laser devices, and new equipment with too little maintenance history are excluded. New equipment follows the manufacturer's recommendations until that history exists."},
{id:"u3l2-3",t:"u3l2",q:"A manager asks which national threshold separates high-risk from medium-risk equipment in a scoring model. What is the correct response?",a:["There is no national threshold; the hospital writes its own bands, publishes them before scoring, applies them uniformly, and gets them approved","The accreditor publishes the thresholds each year","The manufacturer sets them per model","The thresholds are set by the conditions of participation"],c:0,e:"No national table exists. What a surveyor checks is that a written methodology, approved by whoever owns the equipment management plan, was applied by qualified people the same way to every device type, and then followed."},
{id:"u3l2-4",t:"u3l2",q:"A patient event occurs on a model whose incident history had scored lowest on your scale. What should happen to its risk score?",a:["Nothing, because one event is not a trend","Re-score it: incident history rises, which may move the band and return the interval to the manufacturer's","Remove the model from the program entirely","Wait for the annual review"],c:1,e:"Scores are re-examined when the failure history changes, when an incident happens on that model, when the manufacturer changes its guidance, when the fleet size changes, and at the plan's review interval. A score built on thin history is a hypothesis."},
{id:"u3l2-5",t:"u3l2",q:"Your department set a maintenance frequency for a device type under its alternative program and then fell behind, performing the work less often. What is the compliance position?",a:["Acceptable, since the program allows flexibility in frequency","Acceptable if the devices are low risk","A failure, because a hospital must follow the activities and frequencies it set, strictly","Acceptable if the omission is documented"],c:2,e:"An alternative program is a different schedule, never permission to improvise. The hospital must adhere strictly to the activities and frequencies it chose, and equipment in the program must be readily identifiable as such."},

// u3l3
{id:"u3l3-1",t:"u3l3",q:"Three wrong-concentration infusion events occurred this year. Which control should be recommended FIRST?",a:["Retrain the nursing staff on the affected units","Issue a memo to all clinical directors","Add a policy requiring a second-person check","Configure hard concentration limits in the drug library on every pump that supports them"],c:3,e:"Work the hierarchy from the top: eliminate, substitute, engineer, administer, protect. An engineering control such as a hard limit works whether or not anyone is paying attention, while administrative controls hold only while attention lasts."},
{id:"u3l3-2",t:"u3l3",q:"Which element makes a written procedure a control rather than an essay?",a:["A trigger, numbered single actions, roles rather than names, a record, an exception path, an owner, and a review date","A statement of the department's values and goals","A detailed technical description of the device involved","A list of every person who contributed to writing it"],c:0,e:"A control must be followable. Naming roles rather than people lets it survive a resignation, and the exception path tells a person what to do when a step cannot be completed and who to escalate to."},
{id:"u3l3-3",t:"u3l3",q:"When should the measure and baseline for a new control be defined?",a:["After the control has been in place for a quarter","Before the control is implemented","At the next annual plan evaluation","Only if the committee asks for evidence"],c:1,e:"Define the measure and take the baseline before implementation, or you will spend a year arguing about whether anything improved. A control with no measure cannot be defended and cannot be withdrawn."},
{id:"u3l3-4",t:"u3l3",q:"You are reporting device-related use error trends and the actions taken on them. Which committee is MOST appropriate?",a:["The infection control committee","The radiation safety committee","The patient safety committee","The capital committee"],c:2,e:"Device-related events, near misses, use error trends, and alarm issues go to patient safety. Inventory, completion rates, recalls, and the annual plan evaluation go to the environment of care committee, and investigations go to risk management."},
{id:"u3l3-5",t:"u3l3",q:"A staff training record shows an attendance sheet for a new device but no assessments. What is the deficiency?",a:["The attendance sheet should have been countersigned","The record should have been kept in the CMMS","The training was delivered by the wrong person","Training without competency assessment is not a demonstrated control, and an accreditor will read it as a note in a file"],c:3,e:"Competency means the person demonstrated the task, was observed, and the observation was recorded, per person and per equipment type. Attendance proves presence, not capability."},

// u3l4
{id:"u3l4-1",t:"u3l4",q:"A patient was harmed, a device was in the room, and the unit asks whether they can keep using it. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Take the device out of patient use and tag it, leaving everything as found","Test the device against specification to see whether it failed","Pull the event log before anything is disturbed","Notify the manufacturer"],c:0,e:"Removing the device from patient use and tagging it comes before anything else, because a second patient is the only outcome worse than the first. Nothing is cleaned, repaired, recalibrated, updated, or power-cycled until evidence is preserved."},
{id:"u3l4-2",t:"u3l4",q:"Which item is MOST often lost during the first hour of a device incident investigation and matters because of lot numbers?",a:["The device's power cord","The contents of the room's trash, including disposable packaging","The service history printout","The unit's staffing roster"],c:1,e:"Lot numbers of disposables and the packaging that proves what was used live in the trash. Securing the accessories, cables, sensors, disposables, packaging, and the trash is part of preserving the scene."},
{id:"u3l4-3",t:"u3l4",q:"An investigation concludes that a nurse accepted a library default that did not match the bag. Five whys reaches a missing process linking pharmacy formulation changes to drug library updates. Which is the root cause?",a:["The nurse accepted the default without checking","The unit was short-staffed on that shift","There is no process linking a pharmacy formulation change to a drug library update","The pump displays concentration in small type"],c:2,e:"A root cause is a cause whose removal prevents recurrence. The short staffing and the small type are contributing factors that made the event more likely or worse but would not alone have caused it."},
{id:"u3l4-4",t:"u3l4",q:"Where does the complete incident investigation report go, and who may discuss its contents?",a:["To the clinical director, and it may be shared with the unit","To the accreditor at the next survey","To the manufacturer, who leads the analysis","To the risk manager, which is what makes it work product, and its contents are discussed with nobody else"],c:3,e:"The complete report goes to the risk manager, which is what gives it its protected status. A near miss is investigated the same way, and the contents stay inside the investigation."},
{id:"u3l4-5",t:"u3l4",q:"Testing shows the device delivered exactly what was programmed, and the log matches a mis-entered setting. How should the finding be classified and acted on?",a:["Use error; address it with training, configuration, workflow safeguards, and a report to the manufacturer if the design invited the mistake","Device error; replace the unit and check the fleet","No fault found; return the device to service","Device error; report it to the agency within ten work days"],c:0,e:"A use error means the device performed as designed given the input. The fix is education, configuration, and workflow safeguards, plus a report when a confusing design contributed, and both device errors and use errors are trended."},

// u3l5
{id:"u3l5-1",t:"u3l5",q:"A new pump fleet with a new drug library arrives in six weeks and nothing has gone wrong yet. Which tool should the manager use?",a:["Root cause analysis","Failure modes and effects analysis","An incident investigation","A cost of service ratio review"],c:1,e:"FMEA is the proactive tool for failures that have not happened; RCA works backward from an event that already occurred. New equipment, a new or changed process, and a major change are the usual FMEA triggers."},
{id:"u3l5-2",t:"u3l5",q:"A failure mode scores severity 9, occurrence 4, and detection 7. What is the risk priority number?",a:["20","63","252","144"],c:2,e:"RPN is severity times occurrence times detection: 9 x 4 x 7 is 252. The number ranks the list for action; it is not a measurement, so 252 is not twice as dangerous as 126."},
{id:"u3l5-3",t:"u3l5",q:"An FMEA team argues about the detection score. What does a high detection score mean?",a:["The failure is easily caught before it reaches the patient","The failure has severe consequences","The failure occurs frequently","The failure is poorly detected before it reaches the patient"],c:3,e:"A high detection score means detection is poor, which is the axis teams most often reverse. Write the definition on the worksheet before scoring, because a team scoring it backward will spend its budget on the wrong failure mode."},
{id:"u3l5-4",t:"u3l5",q:"After a hard library limit and a second-person check are added, a mode's occurrence and detection scores fall but severity does not. Why?",a:["Severity rarely falls without a design change, because the harm is inherent in the failure","The team scored severity incorrectly the first time","Severity only changes after a re-scoring cycle","Severity is not re-scored in FMEA"],c:0,e:"Occurrence falls when you remove the cause and detection improves when you add a check or alarm. Severity describes the harm the failure causes, so only a design change that alters the effect moves it."},
{id:"u3l5-5",t:"u3l5",q:"A failure mode has a low risk priority number but a severity score of 9. How should the manager treat it?",a:["Remove it from the worksheet, since the ranking is what matters","Keep it on the watch list, because a rare and undetectable fatal failure can rank below a frequent nuisance","Raise its occurrence score so the ranking reflects the danger","Address it only if it recurs"],c:1,e:"RPN ranks by product, so a severe but rare and well-detected failure can sit below a frequent minor one. Read the severity column on its own before accepting any ranked list."},

// u3l6
{id:"u3l6-1",t:"u3l6",q:"A patient died during a procedure and a device was in use. Under the Safe Medical Devices Act of 1990, who must the user facility report to and within what period?",a:["The manufacturer only, within thirty days","The accrediting organization, within twenty-four hours","The FDA and the manufacturer, no later than ten work days after becoming aware","The state health department, within ten calendar days"],c:2,e:"A device-related death is reported to the agency and to the manufacturer no later than ten work days after the facility becomes aware. Where the manufacturer cannot be identified, the report goes to the agency alone."},
{id:"u3l6-2",t:"u3l6",q:"A device-related serious injury occurs and the manufacturer is known. Who must receive the report?",a:["The FDA alone","Nobody, until the annual report is filed","The accrediting organization and the manufacturer","The manufacturer, within ten work days, with copying the agency recommended practice"],c:3,e:"A serious injury is reported to the manufacturer within ten work days, or to the agency instead when the manufacturer cannot be identified. Copying the agency on every suspected serious injury is recommended practice."},
{id:"u3l6-3",t:"u3l6",q:"Which form carries a user facility's annual report and when is it due?",a:["Form FDA 3419, by January 1 of each year","Form FDA 3500A, within ten work days of the last event","Form FDA 3500, at the end of the fiscal year","Form FDA 3419, within thirty days of the last event"],c:0,e:"The annual user facility report goes to the agency on Form FDA 3419 by January 1 each year. Mandatory individual reports use Form FDA 3500A through MedWatch, and Form FDA 3500 is the voluntary form."},
{id:"u3l6-4",t:"u3l6",q:"An investigation is incomplete as the ten work day deadline approaches, and the cause is still uncertain. What should the manager do?",a:["Wait until the cause is established, then file a complete report","File on time on what is known and supplement afterward, because the obligation is triggered by awareness","Ask the manufacturer to file on the hospital's behalf","Record the event internally and file next year's annual report"],c:1,e:"The reporting clock runs from what the facility became aware of, not from what the investigation later concludes. File on the deadline and supplement, rather than missing the deadline while waiting for certainty."},
{id:"u3l6-5",t:"u3l6",q:"A sentinel event has occurred involving a device. What does that trigger, separately from any report to the agency?",a:["An immediate recall of the device model","A ten work day report to the manufacturer only","A full root cause analysis and an action plan on the hospital's own clock","A review at the next annual plan evaluation"],c:2,e:"A sentinel event reaches a patient and results in death, permanent harm, or severe temporary harm. It triggers a full root cause analysis and action plan as an accreditation and patient safety obligation, which filing a regulatory report does not satisfy."},

// u3l7
{id:"u3l7-1",t:"u3l7",q:"A recall notice states there is a reasonable probability that use of the product will cause serious adverse health consequences or death. Which class is it and what does it demand?",a:["Class I, scheduled with the vendor at the next visit","Class II, acted on a planned schedule","Class III, corrected through the normal work queue","Class I, acted on immediately with same-day escalation"],c:3,e:"A Class I recall describes a reasonable probability of serious harm or death. Locate and quarantine or correct immediately, and escalate the same day to risk management and clinical leadership."},
{id:"u3l7-2",t:"u3l7",q:"A notice arrives naming a model and a serial range you may own. What is the FIRST step after logging it?",a:["Query the CMMS by manufacturer, model, and serial or lot range to identify the affected assets","Notify every clinical department in the hospital","Quarantine every unit of that model","Schedule the vendor to perform the correction"],c:0,e:"Identifying the affected devices in the inventory comes first, because recalls name serial and lot ranges. An inventory without manufacturer, model, and serial numbers cannot answer a recall at all."},
{id:"u3l7-3",t:"u3l7",q:"A manufacturer field safety advisory arrives that carries no recall class and requires no action at your hospital. What must happen?",a:["Nothing; advisories without a class need no record","Log it, record what was assessed, what was found, and the decision, even though no action was needed","Forward it to the clinical departments for information only","File it with the manufacturer's service literature"],c:1,e:"Every notice is logged, assessed, and closed with a record, even when the action taken is none. A notice read, judged harmless, and left no trace is indistinguishable from a notice nobody opened."},
{id:"u3l7-4",t:"u3l7",q:"Which two lines does a monthly recall report MOST often omit and MOST need?",a:["Notices received and devices affected","Class III notices and vendor contact names","Recalls still open at month end and the age of the oldest open recall","Devices corrected and the correction cost"],c:2,e:"Totals received and corrected look reassuring on their own. The number still open and the age of the oldest open recall are what a committee and a surveyor actually want to know, so report both every month."},
{id:"u3l7-5",t:"u3l7",q:"Under a Class II recall your records show one unit unaccounted for. Why does that single line matter MOST?",a:["It affects the recall's reported completion percentage","It requires a report to the agency","It delays the vendor's correction schedule","It is the line a surveyor will read, and an unlocated device may still be in patient use"],c:3,e:"A recall closes only when every affected asset has a documented disposition, including units on loan, in storage, in another building, or out with a vendor. An unlocated device may still be on a patient."},

// u3l8
{id:"u3l8-1",t:"u3l8",q:"A set of vital signs appears in the wrong patient's chart, and the monitor, the network, and the record are all working. How should this be handled?",a:["As a data integrity and patient safety event, investigated jointly with IT including the admission, discharge, and transfer feed","As a monitor repair, starting with a performance verification","As a clerical error for the unit to correct","As a network fault for IT alone"],c:0,e:"Integrity means the right value reaching the right patient's record. When nothing is broken, the association between bed and patient is wrong, which makes it a workflow and interface investigation and a patient safety event rather than a clerical one."},
{id:"u3l8-2",t:"u3l8",q:"Your department backs up the CMMS nightly and stores the copies off the system. What is still missing?",a:["A second nightly backup","A tested, documented restore","Encryption of the backup media","A longer retention period"],c:1,e:"A backup nobody has restored is a hope rather than a plan. A dated restore test record with its result is what proves the backup is usable, and it belongs beside version control and audit logs."},
{id:"u3l8-3",t:"u3l8",q:"A vendor will provide remote support into devices whose logs contain patient identifiers. What must be in place before the first session?",a:["A purchase order for the support hours","A confidentiality clause in the service report","A business associate agreement, with safeguards, incident reporting, end-of-contract data handling, and subcontractor obligations","Approval from the clinical department"],c:2,e:"A vendor that creates, receives, maintains, or transmits protected health information for the hospital is a business associate. Remote support into a device holding patient data, a hosted service behind a device, and any arrangement where the vendor removes a device or drive all require the agreement first."},
{id:"u3l8-4",t:"u3l8",q:"A manager is asked which devices in the inventory hold protected health information. What is the acceptable basis for the answer?",a:["An assumption based on device type","An inspection of the device's front panel menus","The clinical department's understanding of the device","Confirmation per model in writing from the manufacturer, recorded in the asset record"],c:3,e:"Usually no is not an answer an auditor accepts. Confirm it per model in writing and record the answer in the asset record, because the list drives wiping before disposal and access control decisions."},
{id:"u3l8-5",t:"u3l8",q:"Eight technicians and three vendor engineers share one service account on a clinical system. What is the MOST appropriate recommendation?",a:["Replace it with unique named accounts under least privilege, with multi-factor authentication where supported","Change the shared password quarterly","Record the shared password in the CMMS for traceability","Restrict the account to the vendor engineers only"],c:0,e:"A shared account identifies nobody, so an audit log against it proves nothing. Unique named accounts, least privilege, multi-factor authentication where supported, and removal of accounts the day somebody leaves are the controls."},

// u3l9
{id:"u3l9-1",t:"u3l9",q:"The security officer asks how many medical devices a new vulnerability bulletin affects. What makes that answerable?",a:["The preventive maintenance schedule","An inventory of networked devices carrying software and firmware versions, operating systems, addresses, segments, and clinical owners","The capital replacement plan","The service contract list"],c:1,e:"Bulletins name versions rather than models, so an inventory that stops at model and serial cannot answer one. Versions, operating systems, segments, patchability, remote access, and the clinical owner are the fields that make the question answerable."},
{id:"u3l9-2",t:"u3l9",q:"A manufacturer states that a vendor patch for a clinical device is not supported and will not be provided. What is the BEST course?",a:["Apply the patch anyway, since the vulnerability is known","Remove the devices from service until a patch exists","Apply compensating controls, document them, have the security officer accept the residual risk, and put a replacement date in the capital plan","Take no action, since the manufacturer has decided"],c:2,e:"An unvalidated patch can take a cleared device out of its cleared configuration. The answer is segmentation, restricted access, hardened configuration, monitoring, and physical security, with the residual risk recorded and an owner and review date attached."},
{id:"u3l9-3",t:"u3l9",q:"Which question about an operating system should be asked in writing before a device is purchased?",a:["Whether the operating system can be replaced by the hospital","How many users the operating system allows","Whether the operating system supports the hospital's antivirus product","Until when the operating system version will be supported, and what happens to the device when that support ends"],c:3,e:"A device with a fifteen-year clinical life on a four-year support horizon is an eleven-year problem bought knowingly. Ask before purchase, record the answer with the asset, and collect the MDS2 as the structured version of those answers."},
{id:"u3l9-4",t:"u3l9",q:"A clinical vendor arrives to install its own wireless access points for a new system. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Treat it as a spectrum and network coordination request before installation","Let the installation proceed and test telemetry afterward","Ask the vendor for the access point model numbers only","Refer the vendor to the clinical department that bought the system"],c:0,e:"Protected telemetry spectrum is licensed and coordinated under the agency rules that manage radio spectrum. An unmanaged transmitter near a telemetry band means dropped waveforms on monitored patients, which is a patient safety event."},
{id:"u3l9-5",t:"u3l9",q:"A networked infusion pump is suspected of compromise while a patient is connected. What is the FIRST action?",a:["Disconnect the device from power to stop the compromise","Isolate it from the network without interrupting patient care, then notify the security officer and IT","Reimage the device from the manufacturer's media","Remove the device and send it to the vendor"],c:1,e:"Isolation preserves care and limits the exposure at the same time; a power cycle can destroy volatile evidence. Then notify security and IT, preserve the device and its logs as in any investigation, bring the manufacturer in, and check the fleet."},

// u3l10
{id:"u3l10-1",t:"u3l10",q:"Normal power is lost in a tower and the unit asks which outlets are on the generator and how long pumps will run. What should already exist?",a:["The vendor contact list","The preventive maintenance completion report","Lists of emergency-branch receptacles by room and of battery devices with tested runtimes","The hospital's capital replacement plan"],c:2,e:"Those questions are answered by lists built in advance: emergency-branch receptacles by room, devices with internal batteries and their tested runtimes, uninterruptible power supply capacity and battery age, and reserve equipment locations."},
{id:"u3l10-2",t:"u3l10",q:"Which utility failure is MOST dangerous because affected devices may keep displaying while no data reaches the chart?",a:["Medical gas","Water","HVAC","The network"],c:3,e:"Power and gas failures are obvious and HVAC degrades slowly. A network failure can leave a monitor showing a waveform while nothing reaches the central station, so a manager must know which devices fail safe, which fail loudly, and which fail silently."},
{id:"u3l10-3",t:"u3l10",q:"Facilities issues a notice of an electrical shutdown in two clinical towers on Saturday morning. What should HTM produce from it?",a:["Rooms and devices affected, battery runtimes, devices to move to the emergency branch, devices to relocate, the published downtime procedure, and the recovery verification plan","An acknowledgment that the department is aware","A request to reschedule the shutdown","A list of vendors to place on standby"],c:0,e:"A utility interruption notice is a clinical event rather than a facilities memo. Turn it into rooms, devices, runtimes, moves, a published downtime procedure, on-site coverage, and a recovery verification plan."},
{id:"u3l10-4",t:"u3l10",q:"Which half of an outage procedure is MOST often missing, and where does most of the harm occur?",a:["The declaration of downtime, at the start of the outage","The recovery procedure, on the way back up","The notification list, during the outage","The after-action review, weeks later"],c:1,e:"Recovery needs the same rigor and usually gets none: who declares the system restored, how downtime data is reconciled into the record, how devices are verified before returning to patients, and who confirms each unit is back on the normal workflow."},
{id:"u3l10-5",t:"u3l10",q:"A drill reveals a wrong phone number, a battery at half its documented runtime, and a reserve monitor out of calibration. What is the MOST appropriate output?",a:["A note in the department file that the drill was completed","A report to the accreditor describing the failures","An after-action review listing what worked, what failed, each change with an owner and a date, and which list or procedure it lands in","A request to repeat the drill immediately"],c:2,e:"A drill is the cheapest place to discover that a list is wrong, and the output is an after-action review whose items carry owners, dates, and the specific list, procedure, or plan section each change updates, with a verification date."}
);
